WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.840
There are very few things Batman doesn't excel
at, and even fewer that he just can't do at

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:05.600
all.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.420
They do exist, however, and there's one character
who can do quite a bit that Batman can't:

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:11.380
the Joker.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.360
Here are the things that the Joker can do
that Batman just can't.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.780
While there have been plenty of stories over
the years where the Dark Knight employs lethal

00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:24.520
methods in his fight against crime, the idea
that Batman doesn't kill people has been a

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.720
pretty solid general rule in comics since
1941.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:35.840
"You won't kill me, out of some misplaced
sense of self-righteousness."

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.480
Not coincidentally, that's about as long as
the Joker's been murdering people by the dozens.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:46.320
In Batman #1, the comic that contains the
Joker's first appearance, the Clown Prince

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:51.260
of Crime manages to murder no fewer than twelve
people through a combination of poison and

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:52.940
good old-fashioned handguns.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:57.920
That pretty much set the tone for the next
75 years, and even though he spent a few years

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.430
focused more on bank robberies and gimmick
crimes, he's more than made up for it in the

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:08.740
years since, with an unofficial count by fans
putting his body count at over 540 on-panel

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:09.540
deaths.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:15.100
It's not just quantity, either, with high-profile
deaths like Jason Todd, the second Robin and

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.620
Sarah Essen, Jim Gordon's second wife.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.280
With all those twos, you'd think Harvey Dent
was the one responsible.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.560
There's no other Gotham City villain with
a body count that even comes close, and the

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:30.060
only villains in the entire DC Universe who
can beat it are the cosmic threats who have

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.640
wiped out entire parallel universes.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.700
Even if Batman was a killer, it's hard to
believe he'd go that far.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:42.240
With all the childhood trauma, self-sacrifice,
and violent confrontations, Batman's brain

00:01:42.251 --> 00:01:46.030
probably isn't exactly a pleasant place for
a mind-reader to be.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:50.980
Still, he's never driven someone insane just
by giving them a peek inside his psyche.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.260
We know someone who has, though.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:58.760
"... hi."

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:03.380
On multiple occasions, the Martian Manhunter
— you know, the guy with all of Superman's

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.360
powers plus he can turn invisible and read
your mind — has been confronted with Joker's

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.220
brain.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.780
It did not go well.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.440
He could barely keep himself sane while poking
around up there.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.160
Considering he can keep the entire Justice
League telepathically linked at once, the

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.749
fact that Joker's brain alone was so harrowing
should tell you just how much his mind affects

00:02:23.749 --> 00:02:25.260
anyone who takes a peek.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.100
Crime never takes a vacation, which means
that Batman doesn't either.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.700
He's so dedicated that he rarely takes a rest,
even when he needs it.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:38.939
Super-criminals, on the other hand, are free
to take as many breaks as they want, assuming

00:02:38.939 --> 00:02:42.769
that they're not busy paying their debt to
society in an asylum that seems to be held

00:02:42.769 --> 00:02:44.600
together with spit and chicken wire.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.240
There's always somebody like the Cluemaster
or the Electrocutioner waiting around to take

00:02:49.241 --> 00:02:51.040
their shot, so why not?

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.849
In fact, we actually know that in the comics
canon, the Joker always makes sure to take

00:02:55.849 --> 00:02:57.460
a vacation once a year.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.500
The catch?

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:04.860
According to Aztek: The Ultimate Man #6, he
only goes somewhere worse than Gotham.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.120
Given the state of the DC Universe in the
late '90s, when Gotham was afflicted with

00:03:09.129 --> 00:03:13.239
two plagues and an earthquake on top of all
the other "regular" supervillain attacks,

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.100
that meant going to some pretty bad places.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.819
And when the Joker takes these little trips,
Batman sees it as his responsibility to bring

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:25.769
him back to justice, meaning that whenever
the Joker takes a vacation, Batman, by definition,

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:27.380
has to work.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:31.940
Say what you will about the Joker, but at
least he keeps his sidekicks alive.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.660
What, too soon?

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:38.319
Look, we hate to say it, but Batman doesn't
have a spotless track record when it comes

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:39.580
to his sidekicks.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:44.480
Jason Todd had his brains bashed in, the Spoiler
was tortured to death with a power drill by

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.329
Black Mask, Bruce Wayne's bodyguard Sasha
Bordeaux was shanked in prison after being

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:51.260
framed as an accessory to murder.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:56.079
Even his own son Damian died, which wound
up driving Batman so crazy that he tracked

00:03:56.079 --> 00:03:59.310
down Frankenstein to figure out how to bring
corpses back to life.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:03.699
Sure, all four of those people came back to
life eventually, but they still went through

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.020
some rough stuff.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.160
But the Joker?

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.900
Believe or not, he's batting a thousand.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:14.060
Harley Quinn is not only alive, she's also
become one of DC's most popular (and marketable)

00:04:14.069 --> 00:04:18.250
characters over the past couple decades, with
multiple starring roles in comics that have

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:21.009
lasted longer than the Joker's ever did.

00:04:21.009 --> 00:04:26.379
Heck, even Gaggy Gagsworthy, a sidekick who
appeared in a single issue back in the '60s,

00:04:26.379 --> 00:04:30.560
is still alive somewhere out there in the
Multiverse — he was in comics as recently

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.099
as 2009!

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:37.220
Batman couldn't keep Robin alive, but the
Joker managed to keep Gaggy around for 40

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:38.040
years!

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.860
Of course, once in a while, the Joker remembers
to be the Joker.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:53.420
Here's one thing you can't take away from
Batman: he's massively popular.

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:58.020
He has been since the start, propelling Detective
Comics to such heights that its publisher

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:02.199
would eventually rename their entire company
"D.C." after it.

00:05:02.199 --> 00:05:07.400
He's starred in thousands of comics, a whole
bunch of movies, cartoons that have been more

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:12.520
or less constant since 1992, and even had
his own breakfast cereal.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:18.160
The Joker, of course, is also popular — arguably
the single most popular villain in the history

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:19.660
of superhero comics.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:25.060
But while Detective has passed 1,000 issues
plus over 800 issues of Batman and not to

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:29.919
mention all those other Bat-centric titles,
the Joker managed to get his only solo series

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.700
canceled after just nine issues.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:38.060
Of course, that was back in 1975, with a pretty
different version of the character than we

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:38.920
have today.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:44.500
It also just wasn't that good, probably because
it's kind of difficult to get behind a remorseless

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:46.920
homicidal maniac as a protagonist.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:51.000
Of course, it didn't help that Batman never
appeared in it, leading the Joker to fight

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.199
some random dude who got hit in the head and
thought he was Sherlock Holmes, but the Joker

00:05:55.199 --> 00:05:56.419
is a proud clown.

00:05:56.420 --> 00:06:00.780
It has to sting a little bit that his only
solo outing couldn't even break double digits.

